strcasecmp, strncasecmp and their _l forms fetch the locale's table of
lowercase once, rather than looking up the locale for each byte, and
tolower_l and toupper_l read the same tables as tolower and toupper.
strstr and memmem are musl's, from ports/mlibc/musl/substring.hpp.

strncasecmp went through the wide characters' case map for each byte of
both strings: it looked up the active locale, compared the locale's
codeset name with "ANSI_X3.4-1968" to choose a character code, decoded
the byte and searched the map. strcasecmp looked up the locale for each
byte as well. In the server benchmark's PHP-FPM row on OptiPlex 1, these
calls were about a quarter of the time php-fpm took over its time on
glibc.

Through the wide characters' map, a byte that is not a whole character,
as every byte above 0x7F is in UTF-8 and in ASCII, became 0, and so did
EOF: strncasecmp("\xc3\xa9" "a", "\xc3\xa9" "b", 3) was 0, and
tolower_l(0xc9, l) was 0. Now every byte maps by the locale's table, as
in glibc. A locale whose table has fewer than the 384 entries, for -128
to 255, changes only ASCII letters.

strstr and memmem compared the needle at each place in the haystack in
turn, which takes the product of the two lengths in the worst case, and
the benchmark's client spent much of its time in strstr. musl's compare a
needle of two to four bytes as one word that slides along the haystack,
and find a longer one by the Two-Way algorithm, in time linear in the
haystack's length.

